From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 21:31:30 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] tplink-cloud-sign: add clean-room C++ signer for
 TP-Link/Mercusys Cloud images

Same tool as package/utils/firmware-utils/patches/001+002 (this
package's PKG_SOURCE_VERSION has drifted from that one's over time,
upstream churn unrelated to this change - see that patch set's own
commit messages for the full rationale), reapplied here against this
Makefile's own pinned commit so `make tools/firmware-utils/compile`
(the actual host-tool build path a developer would use to run this
signer on their own machine) produces it too - the package/utils/
variant alone cross-compiles for the router's target architecture,
not runnable on the build host.

Modern C++17 port of this project's Python prototype (openwrt tree,
branch tools/tplink-cloud-signer, scripts/tplink-cloud-sign.py),
itself a clean-room reimplementation of nm_fwup.c's handle_fw_cloud()
signing side, reverse engineered from TP-Link/Mercusys's own released
GPL sources. Uses only the classic EVP_PKEY/RSA object API rather
than OpenSSL 3.0's provider API, so it builds against LibreSSL too
(tools/firmware-utils's HostBuild stages tools/libressl, not a real
OpenSSL, for host tools).

--private-key is required for `sign` (a key generated by this tool's
own "genkey" subcommand, or --private-key-stdin to read one from
stdin instead of a file - for passing key material through without
writing it to disk or exposing it in argv/`ps`); never TP-Link's real
signing key, which this tool does not have and cannot derive.

Verified byte-identical output against the Python prototype for both
algorithms, cross-checked against the real, compiled vendor verifier,
and confirmed building clean via the real OpenWrt build system on
both paths: package/utils/firmware-utils (cross-compiled, against a
real OpenSSL) and tools/firmware-utils (host-built, against the
LibreSSL tools/firmware-utils's HostBuild actually stages).
